or we can speak now to the shadow  chancellor rachel reeves uh before we  get on to uh those strikes and some of  the labour policy i just want to come  off the back of the interview we've just  had to talk about uh northern ireland  brandon lewis there saying that the  legislation published tomorrow will not  break the law do you believe him  well we haven't seen the legislation yet  but it does look like the government  plan to break international law now this  government seemed to be developing a  record for law breaking and it's not one  that the labour party can support but we  help bring in the good friday agreement  we are deeply passionately committed to  it we want the northern ireland protocol  to work but we know  to to get it to work we have to  negotiate and work with our european  partners now kia stammer was in belfast  and dublin last week talking to leaders  talking to businesses showing the  leadership that frankly is lacking from  the prime minister today  so it's um what exactly uh do you want  to happen then with the northern ireland  protocol  there's some very practical things  sophie that could happen pretty much  straight away the first would be to get  a bespoke veterinary agreement between  the eu and the uk to help sp speed up  the movement of goods and services not  just between northern ireland and the  rest of of britain but also between the  uk and um  and the rest of the european uh union  new zealand has got a veterinary  agreement with the eu it can't be on  beyond the wit of the eu and the uk to  negotiate one of those so you don't have  this huge bureaucracy and delays at the  border which are causing so many many  problems particularly in northern  ireland but also at dover as well so  there are practical things like that  that require statecraft they require a  building of trust and those are the  things that boris johnson has not been  able to do  and those skills and those abilities i  believe that kia stammer has those in  spades  right let's talk about the strikes then  it's something that many of our viewers  are going to be really concerned about  in the weeks and months ahead um  according to the sunday telegraph today  the government's reportedly ready  to remove a legal restriction that was  introduced under tony blair that would  prevent employers hiring agency workers  to cover striking staff would you back  the use of agency workers  it just doesn't make any sense the way  to resolve this is to get people around  the table but the idea that you can  replace a skilled train signaler with an  agency worker would you feel safe going  on a train knowing that it was an agency  worker rather than a properly skilled  and trained up trained signaler i know  that i wouldn't so instead of this uh  fanning the flames and increasing  tensions the government needs to act  like firefighters rather than arsonists  and try and get some resolution to this  because the people that will pay the  price if these strikes go ahead  ordinarily working people who want to go  out to work people who need to get to  doctor's appointment and to  have operations in hospitals businesses  who after everything they've gone  through need their workers in the office  but also people who work in the train  industry will lose pay if they're not  going to work so nobody wants these  strikes to happen the government though  are not an observer in all of this  they're an actor and they need to be  getting people around the table and  resolving these disputes instead of  fanning the flames of the fire um this  week your shadow house secretary west  streeting said put it this way if i was  a member of the rmt and my job was at  risk like this then i would be voting to  go on strike would you be voting to go  on strike  well they've already um they've already  had those those those uh those votes  those decisions made but it's not too  late to avert the strikes and you know  what we're gonna come in  i'm going to have to come in on that  because that really wasn't a straight  answer at all would you be voting to go  on strike if you were an mt worker  well look i want to be the chancellor of  the exchequer sophie that is the job  that i aspire to do and if i was  chancellor of the exchequer today i  would be doing everything within my  power to avert these strikes that's what  people want from a government a  government that is on the side of  ordinary working people trying to uh to  resolve problems rather than to fan the  flames and that's the job that i want to  do i want to be the chancellor of this  great country and if i was chancellor i  would be doing everything i can to avoid  the huge disruption that will come from  strike actions and that's what i want to  see from this prime minister and this  chancellor i spoke to the uh rmt general  secretary lynch on the show a little  while ago and he said i've seen no  response from the labour front bench  that says we support the workers in  their struggles if we just get this  bland democratic party sitting in the  center of politics taking their  instructions off the daily mail not  getting behind worker struggles you have  to ask yourself why do they actually  call themselves the labour party  do you think he has got a point that  you're being a bit delayed  well look the labour party are the party  of working people the clue is in the  name and i'm incredibly proud of the  relationship between the labour party  and our trade unions and and unlike the  um the rail unions we don't want to see  agency workers uh uh coming in who are  not qualified to do jobs are putting  public uh um safety at uh at risk but  look we also want to do everything we  can to avert these strikes and i know  that the people who work in the trade  industry don't want these strikes to  happen either they want a government  that's on their side that tries to  resolve these disputes and as i say  sophie if i was in government today that  would be my number one priority  okay now today you're criticizing the  conservative party for what you're  calling its sticking plaster approach to  deep economic policies  um but just to talk about labour because  i've been asking labour politicians you  know for some weeks now you know what's  the big  transformative idea the vision if you  like from the labour party  annalise dodd says it's to buy make and  sell more in the country  mcfadden told me it's about 200 000  children living in areas with no  outstanding schools lisa nandy said good  clean jobs kierstama talked about a  windfall tax to reduce energy bills  i just can't help thinking you know it's  a reason that everyone seems to give a  different answer to this question  because there isn't a single big  transformative policy from the labour  party  well the great thing about those answers  sophia is it shows that labour party is  buzzing with ideas and indeed labour is  winning the battle of ideas in politics  today you see that with the u-turn from  the government on the windfall tax and i  remember speaking on your program back  in january when i first started calling  for a win for tax and government  ministers said oh we can't do that  because the energy companies are  struggling and it would stop investment  and five months later the government  bowed to the inevitable introduce that  windfall tax and you know if the toys  have run out of ideas and they clearly  have then we've got plenty more of them  as annalisa has said our plans to buy  make and sell more in britain as lisa  says our plans to ensure that every part  of the country has jobs that pay a  decent wage where the government matches  the ambition that people have for their  families and their communities so labor  party are buzzing with ideas under  kier's leadership we are winning uh the  votes of people who turned away from  labour at the last couple of uh  elections and will continue to set out  more of those ideas as i am to doing  today on labour's plans for a stronger  and more secure economy because as well  as that windfall tax which is much  needed to tackle the cost of living  crisis right now we do need more than a  sticking plaster approach we need to be  boosting our energy security our supply  chain security security for businesses  because only by doing those things will  we boost the economic security  of families and family finances right  across the country  now i know you are obviously a  kierstalmer loyalist but ever since of  course he said that he would resign uh  if durham police found against him  there's been lots of leadership  speculation it feels like every time  that we're streeting or lisa and nandy  open their mouths people see it as a  leadership pitch and i was just thinking  before this interview i'm not sure if  anyone's actually asked you the question  um  would you fancy a run at the leadership  at some point if the opportunity were to  arise  but there's not the opportunity is not  there you know kia is the leader of the  labour party i don't know although he  said quite rightly that if he is fined  well i said a moment ago you know i want  to be chancellor of the extract the only  thing i don't like about my job title  today is the shadow at the beginning of  it i want to be the child  i'm absolutely  i'm absolutely not saying first green  chance like that isn't what i want to do  i'm absolutely not saying that you're  not a kid i'm a loyalist and that's why  i sort of introduced a question with  that uh proviso but that is that is  definitely not a uh ruling out is it  but there's just there's no there's  nothing to there's nothing to see here  you know there's no leadership context  but at the point about kia saying that  he would resign if he was fined was to  show the contrast really between  kiyosama a man of honesty integrity of  decency he wants to restore those  values of trust and decency to politics  and public life compared to a rule  breaking a prime minister who has no  respect for the ministerial code uh for  the conduct of of politics and that's  why i'm so proud to uh to stand here as  a member of kia  shadow cabinet and why i'm so desperate  to get rid of this prime minister with a  bluster and the failure to rise to the  challenges of the country and at the  same time is lying and cheating uh that  is is  so discrediting this government and our  country  okay rachel reeves uh thank you very  much indeed rachel ruth says hello  chancellor thank you

